Thinking: The user asks: "I want contour lines on my MapLibre GL JS map, generated at runtime from a raster-dem elevation source, rather than pre-rendering a separate vector contour tileset ahead of time. Is this possible, and how would I approach it?" We need to answer using the skill. Provide explanation that yes, using maplibre-contour library, create DemSource, set up, add vector source with contour protocol URL, add line layer, etc. Also mention steps, encoding, thresholds, etc. Provide code snippets. Also mention that MapLibre itself doesn't have built-in contour generation; need external library. Provide details about performance, worker, etc. Also mention alternative: pre-generate if needed. Provide references. Ensure we follow skill guidelines.

### Short answer  
Yes – you can generate contour lines **on‑the‑fly** from a `raster-dem` source with the **`maplibre‑contour`** library. The library runs the marching‑squares algorithm in a Web Worker, creates a virtual vector tileset that MapLibre can consume, and lets you style the contours just like any other vector layer.

Below is a complete, step‑by‑step guide (including a minimal working example) that shows how to:

1. **Add a raster‑DEM source** (the same source you would use for hillshade, color‑relief, or 3D terrain).  
2. **Create a `DemSource`** from `maplibre‑contour`.  
3. **Register the contour protocol** with the map.  
4. **Add a vector source** that points at the generated contour tiles.  
5. **Add a line (or label) layer** to draw the contours.  

---

## 1️⃣ Prerequisites

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6** | `color‑relief` and the `sky` object were added in 5.6; `maplibre‑contour` works with any recent 5.x release. |
| **A raster‑DEM source** (e.g. Terrarium, Mapbox, or `terrain-rgb`) | The library reads the raw elevation values from the DEM tiles. |
| **`maplibre‑contour` package** (npm or a CDN build) | Provides the `DemSource` class and the contour‑protocol URL generator. |
| **A modern browser** (Web Worker + `OffscreenCanvas` support) | The contour work is done off‑main‑thread. |

```bash
# npm / yarn
npm install maplibre-gl maplibre-contour
# or, if you prefer a CDN:
# <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
# <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.js"></script>
```

---

## 2️⃣ Add the DEM source (the same one you’ll use for terrain/hillshade)

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 45,               // optional – you can keep it flat for 2‑D contours
  antialias: true
});

// 2️⃣ Add the raster‑DEM source (you can also declare it in the style JSON)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // <-- your DEM tiles
  tileSize: 256,
  // encoding must match the DEM you serve:
  //   'terrarium' | 'mapbox' | 'terrain-rgb' (default is mapbox)
  //   See https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/sources/#raster-dem
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

> **Tip:** If you already have a `raster-dem` source in your style JSON, you can skip the `addSource` call and just reference its `id` later.

---

## 3️⃣ Create a `DemSource` (the contour engine)

```javascript
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';   // or window.maplibreContour if using a CDN

// 3️⃣ Build a DemSource that knows where the DEM tiles live
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL pattern must be the same as the raster‑dem source above
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // Must match the DEM encoding you declared on the raster‑dem source
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // The highest zoom level you expect to request contours for.
  // The library will request DEM tiles up to this zoom.
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Set to true to run the heavy work in a Web Worker (recommended)
  worker: true
});

// 4️⃣ Hook the DemSource into MapLibre – this registers a custom protocol
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

### What `setupMaplibre` does
* Registers a **`contour://`** protocol handler that the map can request as a vector tile URL.  
* Spins up a Web Worker (if `worker:true`) that decodes DEM tiles, runs marching‑squares, and returns GeoJSON‑encoded vector tiles.  

---

## 4️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that points at the contour protocol

```javascript
// 5️⃣ Create a vector source that uses the contour protocol URL generator
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is generated per‑zoom level; the library builds it for you.
  // You can pass options that control contour spacing, attribute names, etc.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour spacing (in metres) per zoom level ────────────────────────
      //   The library will emit two sets of lines: "minor" and "major".
      thresholds: {
        // zoom: [minorSpacing, majorSpacing]
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        14: [50, 200]
      },
      // ── Property names that will appear on each feature ───────────────────
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // elevation of the line (in metres)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 0 = minor, 1 = major (useful for styling)
      // ── Optional: give the layer a deterministic name ───────────────────
      contourLayer: 'contours' // this becomes the source‑layer name
    })
  ],
  // The vector source must be able to serve up to the highest zoom you need.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

**Explanation of the options**

| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `thresholds` | A map of *zoom → [minor, major]* spacing (metres). The library will generate two contour levels at each zoom. Choose tighter spacing at higher zooms for more detail. |
| `elevationKey` | Name of the numeric property that holds the contour elevation. Use this in a style expression if you want a data‑driven color ramp. |
| `levelKey` | Name of the property that tells you whether the line is a “minor” (0) or “major” (1) contour. Handy for line‑width or label styling. |
| `contourLayer` | The name of the *source‑layer* inside the virtual vector tileset. You’ll reference this in `source-layer` when adding a layer. |

---

## 5️⃣ Add a line layer (and optionally a label layer)

```javascript
// 6️⃣ Draw the contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  // The source‑layer name must match the `contourLayer` you passed above.
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  paint: {
    // Light gray for minor contours, darker for major ones
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, '#222222',   // major
      '#bbbbbb'       // minor (default)
    ],
    // Thicker line for major contours
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 1.5,
      0.8
    ],
    // Optional: make the lines semi‑transparent so underlying imagery shows through
    'line-opacity': 0.7
  }
});

// 7️⃣ (Optional) Add labels that show the elevation value
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  layout: {
    // Show a label only on major contours (level === 1)
    'text-field': [
      'case',
      ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
      ''
    ],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222222',
    'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

**Result:** As you pan/zoom the map, the worker will request the needed DEM tiles, compute the contours for the current view, and feed them to MapLibre as vector tiles. The line and label layers update automatically—no pre‑generated contour tileset required.

---

## 6️⃣ Performance & best‑practice notes

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---------|----------------|
| **Worker overhead** | Keep `worker:true` (default) – it moves the heavy marching‑squares work off the UI thread. |
| **Tile‑fetching** | The library only requests DEM tiles up to `maxzoom` you set on the `DemSource`. If you need contours at zoom 15, make sure your DEM source actually provides tiles that deep (or set `maxzoom` lower). |
| **Contour density** | Very fine spacing (e.g., 10 m) at high zoom can generate thousands of line segments and hurt FPS. Use coarser spacing at lower zooms (`thresholds` map) and tighten only where you need detail. |
| **Memory** | The worker caches decoded DEM tiles; on low‑memory devices you may want to limit `maxzoom` or clear the cache manually (`demSource.clearCache()`). |
| **Styling** | Because the contours are a *vector* source, you can use any MapLibre expression (including `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["get", "ele"], …]`) to color them by elevation. |
| **Compatibility** | `maplibre-contour` works with MapLibre GL JS **5.x** and later. It does **not** work with Mapbox GL JS 1.x (different style spec). |

---

